---
// Short framing before any result: what a buoy can and cannot tell you about a surf break.
import metadata from "../../../../public/data/surf-forecast/metadata.json";
import { SURF_BREAKS, uniqueBuoyStations } from "../../../lib/surf-forecast/spots";

const months = metadata.window.months;
const pairs = metadata.model_pairs.toLocaleString();
---

<section id="approach" class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-16 md:px-12 md:py-20">
	<h2 class="font-anton text-4xl md:text-5xl">A buoy is not a surf break</h2>
	<div class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		<p>
			This is the limitation worth stating before any result, not after. A buoy floats in deep water kilometres
			offshore and measures open-ocean swell. What you actually ride depends on refraction, bathymetry, shadowing
			and tide, and no buoy measures any of that. There is no free, public, archived record of observed surf height
			at a break anywhere, so this checks forecasts against the {uniqueBuoyStations().length} buoys that sit closest
			to {SURF_BREAKS.length} breaks across Hawaii, Northern California and Southern California, and says plainly
			which breaks each buoy can speak for.
		</p>
		<p>
			The forecasts come from Open-Meteo's archive of what each wave model predicted one to seven days before every
			hour of the last {months} months. That archive is what makes this possible: instead of collecting forecasts
			for a few weeks and waiting, it gives {pairs} forecasts scored against a buoy reading taken within 30 minutes
			of the hour they were for.
		</p>
	</div>
</section>
